type: requirement-spec
id: unassisted-recovery
---
# Unassisted recovery

## Applies to

**Targets:** [Restore a document](../use-cases/restore.use-case.spec.md).

### Population

Workspace members who have completed normal onboarding but have never used recovery.

### Conditions

Each member has accidentally deleted a document they recognize and has permission
to restore it within its retention window. They use a supported desktop browser
with a working network connection. Ordinary in-product help is available; no
facilitator or colleague provides recovery instructions.

### Exclusions

Expired copies, unsupported browsers, and recovery during a network interruption.
These exclusions limit this requirement; other restoration requirements still apply.

## Statement

Members in the stated context must be able to recover the intended document
without external assistance at the success rate defined in [Criterion](#criterion).

## Criterion

### Measure

The proportion of eligible task attempts in which the member restores the intended
document and opens the recovered contents without external assistance. Count one
attempt per member; an abandoned attempt or facilitator intervention is a failure.
Divide successful attempts by all eligible attempts, including failures.

### Target

At least 90%.

## Supports

[Recover valuable work](../jobs/recover.job.spec.md).

## Rationale

Recovery should let members resume work without waiting for support. This fictional
example uses an illustrative bound; a real product would justify it through user
research and stakeholder agreement. A response-time measurement alone would not
establish successful recovery.

## Evidence

### Subject

The candidate recovery workflow, with implementation revision recorded before the study.

### Basis

[Statement](#statement), [Criterion](#criterion), and [Applies to](#applies-to).

### Participants

Members matching the specified population, observed by a facilitator who does not
provide recovery instructions.

### Method

Recruit 30 eligible members, each attempting one recovery in a seeded workspace.
Balance recruitment across supported desktop browsers and include members with
different levels of general workspace experience. Record all attempts and compute
the proportion using the criterion's definition. Report the sample size, uncertainty,
and recruitment limitations alongside the comparison; the sample is not proof of
success for every user or environment.

### Success criteria

Compare the observed proportion with the required bound in [Criterion](#criterion).
Retain the comparison and its limitations for the assessment decision.

### Conditions

Use a working network and supported browsers, with normal in-product help enabled.
Give each member a recognizable document to delete before the recovery task.
Explain how the seeded task represents the context in [Applies to](#applies-to)
and record deviations without silently changing the requirement's scope.

### Evidence to retain

Retain the implementation revision, recruitment criteria, browser and task setup,
attempt outcomes, interventions, calculation and assessment limitations. Record
the assessment conclusion separately from this plan.
-- specs/document-workspace.system.spec.md --
---
type: system-spec
---
# Document workspace

## Purpose

Help teams preserve and recover valuable work.

## Scope

Document deletion and restoration within a workspace.

## Goals

### Reduce lost work

Reduce the amount of valuable work lost through accidental deletion.

### Reduce retention cost

Reduce unnecessary storage cost without shortening the recovery commitment.

## Environment

Mobile clients sometimes lose connectivity. The service must accommodate retries after reconnection; connectivity loss is an operating condition, not a premise that excuses restoration requirements.

## Framing

Explore recovery workflows.

### Working idea

Could a restore preview help?

## Out of scope

Recovering documents after their retained copies expire. External backup restoration has a separate boundary.

## Project references

[Delivery and review plan](../project-plan.md) owns milestones and stakeholder review responsibilities.

## Interacts with

[Platform clock](../clock/specs/platform-clock.system.spec.md) supplies time readings across the workspace boundary.

## Required interfaces

### Time readings

**ID:** time-readings

Read UTC time for retention comparisons.

#### Counterpart

[Platform clock](../clock/specs/platform-clock.system.spec.md).

#### Contracts

[Time API](../contracts/time.openapi.yaml) is the authoritative wire contract.

## Provided interfaces

### Restoration requests

**ID:** restoration-requests

Accept restoration requests from authorized clients. The native contract remains to be selected.

## Assumptions

### Trusted time

The platform supplies a trustworthy UTC clock for retention calculations.

#### Basis

The [clock observation](#clock-observation) motivates this premise; it does not establish a permanent guarantee.

#### Applicability

Retention calculations in the managed production environment while the platform clock service meets its stated operating conditions.

#### Basis

##### Subject

The premise that production clock offset stays within the agreed tolerance, and the justification for relying on it at the recovery boundary.

##### Basis

[Clock observation](#clock-observation) and the [recovery window](business-rules/retention.business-rule.spec.md).

##### Participants

Platform operators and the retention reviewer.

##### Conditions

The managed production environment, including clock resynchronization events.

##### Method

Compare platform time readings to an independent reference before deployment and during operation.

##### Success criteria

Offset remains within the retention boundary's agreed tolerance. The tolerance is still an open question.

## Evidence

### Clock observation

This example uses an invented observation to illustrate evidence linking. Replace it with actual measurements before relying on the premise.

#### Type

Illustrative hypothesis.

## Open questions

### Clock tolerance

What time offset can the recovery-window boundary tolerate?

#### Raised by

Workspace requirements reviewer.

#### Raised on

2026-11-11.

#### Resolve by

Before acceptance of retention-boundary tests.

#### Importance

Serious: affects decisions near the recovery deadline.

#### Evidence needed

Clock offset measurements and stakeholder agreement on the boundary policy.

#### Tracking reference

[Boundary decision](../project-plan.md#retention-boundary-decision) owns resolution.

### External backup handoff

Who owns the handoff after retained copies expire?

## Glossary

### Retained document

A deleted document kept inside its recovery window.

#### Alternative names

- Recovery copy

### Recovery copy

An external backup copy. This deliberate alias collision needs review.

## Description

A recovery window limits restoration from retained state; it does not describe external backup guarantees.

### Implementation option

A background worker could expire retained copies. This suggestion does not prescribe a scheduler or database.

### Example

A member restores a document one day after deletion and resumes editing.

## Concept uses

### DocumentTrash

#### Definition

[Trash](concepts/trash.concept.spec.md)

#### Supports

[Recover lost work](jobs/recover.job.spec.md).

#### Relies on

[Trusted time](#trusted-time) bounds retention intervals.

## Evidence

### Subject

The proposed workspace recovery workflow in a prototype, then the identified release build; this assesses intended use rather than only conformance to the written recovery rule.

### Basis

[Recover lost work](jobs/recover.job.spec.md) and [Reduce lost work](#reduce-lost-work).

### Participants

Representative workspace members, including mobile users, and a stakeholder who owns the recovery policy.

### Method

Observe representative users attempting recovery with realistic document collections; discuss where the proposed workflow fails their intended progress.

### Success criteria

Participants recover the intended document and can resume their interrupted task without specialist assistance. Record exceptions and limits rather than generalizing to every user.

### Conditions

Representative desktop and mobile sessions, including reconnection after intermittent connectivity; record prototype or build revision and participant context.

### Cases

#### Resume interrupted work

Ask a participant to recover a mistakenly deleted document and resume the task.

##### Expected result

The participant identifies the intended document, recovers it, and resumes the task.

##### Evidence to retain

Consented observation notes, task outcome, revision, conditions and observed obstacles.

### Evidence to retain

Retain the protocol and observations separately from the assessment of intended-use fitness and any stakeholder acceptance decision. This example records a plan only.
-- specs/use-cases/restore.use-case.spec.md --
---
type: use-case-spec
---
# Restore a document

## Goal

Recover an accidentally deleted document and resume work.

## System boundary

**Subject system:** [Document workspace](../document-workspace.system.spec.md)

The workspace owns retention and restoration; external backups are outside this boundary.

## Supports

[Recover lost work](../jobs/recover.job.spec.md).

## Involves

[DocumentTrash](../document-workspace.system.spec.md#documenttrash).

## Main success scenario

1. The member selects a retained document.
2. The workspace restores it according to [Restore retained content](../requirements/restore-content.requirement.spec.md).
   - **Illustrates:** [Restore retained content](../requirements/restore-content.requirement.spec.md), [Restore](../concepts/trash.concept.spec.md#restore).
3. The member resumes work.

## Success guarantees

The member can resume work on the restored document.

## Authority

The use case specifies the interaction. [Restore retained content](../requirements/restore-content.requirement.spec.md) specifies the recovery behavior.

## Primary actor

An authorized workspace member.

## Goal level

User task.

## Sequence

The scenario order is representative; the recovery requirement governs restoration.

## Extensions

### The recovery deadline has passed

The workspace declines restoration from retained state and explains that the recovery window has ended.

### The member lacks authorization

The workspace declines restoration and leaves the retained document unchanged.

## Evidence

### Subject

The proposed interaction and alternatives; assess whether they support members in resuming interrupted work.

### Basis

[Recover lost work](../jobs/recover.job.spec.md).

### Participants

Workspace members and the recovery-policy stakeholder.

### Method

Review examples and a prototype with the participants, including counterexamples and unsupported situations.

### Success criteria

The participants can explain how the proposal addresses recovery needs, and unresolved mismatches are recorded for revision.

### Conditions

Representative accidental-deletion situations and the stated recovery-window boundary.

### Evidence to retain

Keep example responses and observations, the reviewed revision, the assessment rationale and any disagreements. Record acceptance separately.

-- specs/requirements/request-identity.requirement.spec.md --
---
type: requirement-spec
id: request-identity
---
# Preserve request identity

## Statement

A retried restoration request must retain its original request identifier.

## Applies to

**Targets:** [Restoration requests](../document-workspace.system.spec.md#restoration-requests).

## Rationale

Repeat a request after an interrupted response and inspect the retained identifier.

The interaction contract covers retries; the native protocol remains to be selected.

-- specs/requirements/restore-content.requirement.spec.md --
---
type: requirement-spec
id: restore-content
---
# Restore retained content

## Statement

When an authorized workspace member requests restoration before the 30-day recovery period ends, the workspace must restore the retained document content and metadata.

## Derived from

[Recovery window](../business-rules/retention.business-rule.spec.md) supplies the time boundary.

## Supports

[Recover lost work](../jobs/recover.job.spec.md).

## Applies to

**Targets:** [DocumentTrash](../document-workspace.system.spec.md#documenttrash)

## Rationale

Exercise restoration with retained content and metadata; compare both to their pre-deletion values. Include requests immediately before the recovery deadline.
